✅ What is SOLID?
SOLID is a set of 5 OOP design principles for cleaner, flexible, maintainable code:

| Letter | Principle                         |
| ------ | --------------------------------- |
| **S**  | Single Responsibility Principle   |
| **O**  | Open/Closed Principle             |
| **L**  | Liskov Substitution Principle     |
| **I**  | Interface Segregation Principle   |
| **D**  | Dependency Inversion Principle    |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
A class should have only **one reason to change** — one job.

❌ Bad:

```java
class UserService {
    public void registerUser() { }
    public void sendEmail() { } // email is not UserService's job
}
```

✅ Good:

```java
class UserService {
    public void registerUser() { }
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail() { }
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Open for **extension**, closed for **modification**.
Add new behavior without changing existing working code.

```java
interface Notification {
    void send(String message);
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Email: " + message);
    }
}

class SMSNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS: " + message);
    }
}

class NotificationService {
    public void notifyUser(Notification notification, String message) {
        notification.send(message);
    }
}
```

➡ Add `PushNotification` / `SlackNotification` without changing `NotificationService`.
(Uses polymorphism + interfaces.)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
Subtypes must be **substitutable**(changeable) for their base types.
A subclass should not break expectations of the parent type.

❌ Bad:

```java
class Bird {
    void fly() {}
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Ostrich can't fly!");
    }
}
```

✅ Good:

```java
interface Bird {
    void eat();
}

interface FlyingBird extends Bird {
    void fly();
}

class Parrot implements FlyingBird {
    public void fly() { }
    public void eat() { }
}

class Ostrich implements Bird {
    public void eat() { } // no fake fly()
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
Don't force a class to implement methods it doesn't need.
Prefer many small interfaces over one fat interface.

❌ Bad:

```java
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

class Robot implements Worker {
    public void work() {}
    public void eat() {} // robots don't eat
}
```

✅ Good:

```java
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

class Human implements Workable, Eatable {
    public void work() {}
    public void eat() {}
}

class Robot implements Workable {
    public void work() {}
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
Depend on **abstractions**, not concrete implementations.
High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules — both depend on interfaces.

❌ Bad:

```java
class MySQLDatabase {
    public void connect() {}
}

class Application {
    private MySQLDatabase db = new MySQLDatabase(); // tightly coupled
}
```

✅ Good (pure Java / DIP):

```java
interface Database {
    void connect();
}

class MySQLDatabase implements Database {
    public void connect() {}
}

class PostgresDatabase implements Database {
    public void connect() {}
}

class Application {
    private final Database db;

    public Application(Database db) { // inject abstraction
        this.db = db;
    }
}
```

➡ Swap MySQL / Postgres without changing `Application`.

🌱 Spring tip (how DIP shows up in DI):
When **multiple** beans implement the same interface:

| Situation                  | What Spring does      |
| -------------------------- | --------------------- |
| One implementation         | Auto-inject           |
| Multiple implementations   | Ambiguity error       |
| `@Primary`                 | Default choice        |
| `@Qualifier("beanName")`   | Explicit choice       |
| `@Profile("dev")`          | Environment-based     |

```java
class Application {
    private final Database db;

    public Application(@Qualifier("mySQLDatabase") Database db) {
        this.db = db;
    }
}
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Summary Table

| Principle | Meaning                                      |
| --------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| **S**     | One class = one responsibility               |
| **O**     | Extend without modifying existing code       |
| **L**     | Subclass must behave like the parent type    |
| **I**     | Keep interfaces small and focused            |
| **D**     | Depend on interfaces, not concrete classes   |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) SRP ≠ “one method only” — it means **one reason to change** / one responsibility.
2) OCP is often achieved with **interfaces + polymorphism**, not by never editing files forever.
3) LSP is broken if subclass throws / no-ops parent methods it cannot honor (`Ostrich.fly()`).
4) ISP ≠ DIP: ISP = split fat interfaces; DIP = depend on abstractions.
5) Constructor injection of a service interface in Spring = practical **DIP + composition/delegation**.
